[200.002617,"o","\u001b[?25l\u001b[2;133H\u001b[1K\r    \u001b[2m  \"query\": \"IL6ST hyper IgE syndrome pneumatocele\",\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[3;133H\u001b[1K\u001b[2m\r    … +49 lines (ctrl + t to view transcript)\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[4;133H\u001b[1K\r    \u001b[2m  ]\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[5;133H\u001b[1K\r    \u001b[2m}\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[6;133H\u001b[1K\u001b[32m\u001b[1m\r\n•\u001b(B\u001b[m \u001b[1mRan\u001b(B\u001b[m \u001b[38;5;111mdx-pubmed\u001b[38;5;189m search\u001b[38;5;103m --\u001b[38;5;217mquery\u001b[38;5;189m \u001b[38;5;151m\"ZNF341 hyper IgE syndrome facial features\"\u001b[38;5;103m --\u001b[38;5;217mabstracts\u001b[39m\u001b[47X\u001b[8;133H\u001b[1K\u001b[2m\r  └ {\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[9;133H\u001b[1K\r    \u001b[2m  \"query\": \"ZNF341 hyper IgE syndrome facial features\",\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[10;133H\u001b[1K\u001b[2m\r    … +82 lines (ctrl + t to view transcript)\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[11;133H\u001b[1K\r    \u001b[2m  ]\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[12;133H\u001b[1K\r    \u001b[2m}\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[13;133H\u001b[1K\u001b[2m\r\n• \u001b[7mI'm looking into IL6ST variants and their relationship with STAT3, particularly how they can lead to \"original hyper-IgE syndrome.\"\u001b(B\u001b[m\r\n  \u001b[2m\u001b[7mI wonder if there's a way to differentiate the cases related to IL6ST, especially since dysmorphic facies could also be involved.\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[2X\r\n  \u001b[2m\u001b[7mIt's interesting to consider the implications of these genetic factors and how they might manifest in different individuals. Let's\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[1X\n\u001b[1K\r  \u001b[2m\u001b[7msee what more I can find on this topic!\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[18;133H\u001b[1K\n\u001b[1K\r  \u001b[1m\u001b[2m\u001b[7mEvaluating differential diagnoses\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[20;133H\u001b[1K\r\n  \u001b[2m\u001b[7mThis seems like a solid plan. The differential includes Chronic Granulomatous Disease (CGD), but I wonder if it’s a more nuanced\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[3X\r\n  \u001b[2m\u001b[7mclinical picture. Maybe I need to consider IL6ST/ZNF341 either as a separate differential or grouped with others. If I were to rank\u001b(B\u001b[m\r\n  \u001b[2m\u001b[7mthem, I think I could go with: 1) DOCK8 deficiency, 2) IL6ST/ZNF341-related hyper-IgE syndrome, and 3) Chronic Granulomatous\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[7X\u001b[24;133H\u001b[1K\r  \u001b[2m\u001b[7mDisease. Overall, this feels coherent.\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[25;133H\u001b[1K\n\u001b[1K\r  \u001b[1m\u001b[2m\u001b[7mCrafting concise diagnoses\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[27;133H\u001b[1K\r\n  \u001b[2m\u001b[7mI'm comparing top diagnoses, including DOCK8 and mentioning Chronic Granulomatous Disease (CGD). I should list genetics,\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[11X\r\n  \u001b[2m\u001b[7mparticularly focusing on IL6ST/ZNF341, noting DOCK8 as a potential differential. Maybe I can state, \"The best single diagnosis is\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[2X\r\n  \u001b[2m\u001b[7mSTAT3-related autosomal dominant hyper-IgE syndrome 1 (Job syndrome; STAT3, OMIM 147060)\" while including other possibilities like\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[1X\r\n  \u001b[2m\u001b[7mIL6ST and ZNF341. I really want to keep the bullets concise but informative, aiming for four key points without overcrowding the\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[3X\u001b[32;133H\u001b[1K\r  \u001b[2m\u001b[7mparagraph.\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[33;133H\u001b[1K\u001b[2m\r\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[35;133H\u001b[1K\n\u001b[1K\u001b[2m\r• \u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[1mLeading diagnosis\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[37;133H\u001b[1K\r\n  The best fit is \u001b[1mSTAT3-related autosomal dominant hyper-IgE syndrome 1\u001b(B\u001b[m (\u001b[1mJob syndrome\u001b(B\u001b[m; \u001b[1mSTAT3\u001b(B\u001b[m, \u001b[1mOMIM 147060\u001b(B\u001b[m), with moderate confidence.\r\n  The overall pattern is the classic “original hyper-IgE syndrome” frame rather than a nonspecific atopic or phagocyte-defect\u001b[8X\u001b[40;133H\u001b[1K\r  presentation.\u001b[41;133H\u001b[1K\n\u001b[1K\r  \u001b[1mWhy it fits\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[43;133H\u001b[1K\r\n  - \u001b[1mEczematoid dermatitis, very high IgE, and eosinophilia\u001b(B\u001b[m form the core immunologic pattern of hyper-IgE syndrome.\u001b[18X\r\n  - \u001b[1mRecurrent Staphylococcus aureus skin abscesses\u001b(B\u001b[m plus \u001b[1mrecurrent pneumonia with pulmonary pneumatoceles\u001b(B\u001b[m are especially\u001b[14X\u001b[46;133H\u001b[1K\r    characteristic of the classic Job-syndrome phenotype.\r\n  - \u001b[1mCoarse facial features and a prominent forehead\u001b(B\u001b[m are important nonimmune clues that push this away from simpler infection-prone\u001b[3X\u001b[48;133H\u001b[1K\r    immunodeficiencies and toward \u001b[1mSTAT3-HIES\u001b(B\u001b[m.\r\n  - The phenotype views repeatedly kept the case inside the \u001b[1mhyper-IgE / STAT3-pathway family\u001b(B\u001b[m, with \u001b[1mSTA"]
[200.002755,"o","T3, IL6ST, DOCK8, and ZNF341\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[3X\r\n    all surfacing, which is exactly the family structure expected for this syndrome.\u001b[49X\r\n  - The \u001b[1mdecreased neutrophil oxidative burst\u001b(B\u001b[m is the main discordant clue, but by whole-case fit it is not strong enough to outweigh\u001b[2X\r\n    the far more classic Job-syndrome combination of facies, eczema, staphylococcal abscesses, and pneumatoceles.\u001b[20X\u001b[53;133H\u001b[1K\n\u001b[1K\r  \u001b[1mWhy the runner-up lost\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[55;133H\u001b[1K\r\n  The closest runner-up is \u001b[1mDOCK8 deficiency\u001b(B\u001b[m. It stayed high because it also explains eczema, eosinophilia, elevated IgE, recurrent\u001b[3X\r\n  infections, and abnormal T-cell subsets. What moved this case toward \u001b[1mSTAT3-HIES\u001b(B\u001b[m instead was the more classic \u001b[1mJob-syndrome\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[10X\r\n  \u001b[1mmorphology plus pneumatocele pattern\u001b(B\u001b[m; those features are much more characteristic of \u001b[1mSTAT3-type hyper-IgE syndrome\u001b(B\u001b[m than of DOCK8\u001b[3X\r\n  deficiency. The oxidative-burst abnormality kept chronic granulomatous disease alive as a separate alternate, but CGD did not\u001b[6X\u001b[60;133H\u001b[1K\r  explain the full syndrome nearly as well.\u001b[61;133H\u001b[1K\n\u001b[1K\r  \u001b[1mRanked differential\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[63;133H\u001b[1K\r\n  - \u001b[1mDOCK8 deficiency (hyper-IgE syndrome 2)\u001b(B\u001b[m - strong immune overlap, especially with T-cell abnormalities, but weaker fit for\u001b[8X\u001b[65;133H\u001b[1K\r    characteristic facies and pneumatoceles.\u001b[66;133H\u001b[1K\n\u001b[1K\u001b[48;5;238m\r\n \u001b[132X\u001b[1m\r\n›\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[48;5;238m \u001b[2mImplement {feature}\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[48;5;238m\u001b[112X\r\n \u001b[132X\u001b[49m\u001b[71;133H\u001b[1K\u001b[2m\r  gpt-5.4 xhigh · /demo/miniOdyssey\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[?12l\u001b[?25h\u001b[66;1H  - \u